NAME
       grep - print lines that match patterns

SYNOPSIS
       grep [OPTION]... PATTERN [FILE]...
       grep [OPTION]... -e PATTERN... [FILE]...
       egrep ...  (same as grep -E)
       fgrep ...  (same as grep -F)

DESCRIPTION
       Search each FILE (standard input when none, the current directory
       with -r) for lines matching PATTERN and print them. PATTERN is a POSIX basic
       regular expression by default. On a terminal, matches are highlighted in
       red, file names in magenta and line numbers in green.

OPTIONS
       -E
           PATTERN is an extended regular expression: + ? | ( ) { } are special

       -F
           PATTERN is a fixed string

       -G
           PATTERN is a basic regular expression (default)

       -e PATTERN
           use PATTERN; may be given several times

       -f FILE
           read patterns from FILE, one per line

       -i, --ignore-case
           ignore case distinctions

       -v, --invert-match
           select non-matching lines

       -w, --word-regexp
           match only whole words

       -x, --line-regexp
           match only whole lines

       -c, --count
           print only a count of matching lines per file

       -l, --files-with-matches
           print only names of files with matches

       -L, --files-without-match
           print only names of files without matches

       -n, --line-number
           prefix each line with its line number

       -b
           prefix each line with its byte offset

       -o, --only-matching
           print only the matched parts of lines

       -h, -H
           suppress or force the file name prefix

       -r, -R, --recursive
           search directories recursively

       -q, --quiet
           print nothing; exit 0 on the first match

       -s
           suppress error messages about unreadable files

       -m NUM
           stop after NUM matching lines

       -A NUM, -B NUM, -C NUM, -NUM
           print NUM lines of trailing, leading or surrounding context

       --color[=WHEN]
           highlight matches: always, auto (default) or never

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
       .  any character        [abc] [^abc] [a-z] [[:digit:]]  bracket expressions
       ^ $  start and end of line   *  zero or more     \+ \?  (BRE) / + ?  (ERE)
       \{m,n\} (BRE) / {m,n} (ERE)  repetition     \( \) (BRE) / ( ) (ERE)  group
       \| (BRE) / | (ERE)  alternation    \1..\9  back reference
       \w \W \s \S \d \D  word, space and digit classes
       \b \B \< \>  word boundaries

EXIT STATUS
       0 if a line was selected, 1 if no lines were selected, 2 if an error occurred.

EXAMPLES
       grep -rn TODO /root/work
       grep -E '^(int|void) ' main.c
       ps | grep sh
       grep -c '' file   (count lines)

SEE ALSO
       find(1), less(1), sort(1)
